{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgoAciUeuurtJkQmtWeRn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The held-out color pairs show no effect, at -0.19, while the training ones come in at +4.52. So the effect seems tied to the color items I trained on, which include eggplant, lime, lemon, crow, frog, and polar at 100% detection in the layer-0 subspace. That specificity would fit a memorization effect, since the model could be learning particular subject-to-answer pairs. Now I'm looking at which heads are involved, starting with L12 h2 and L15 h3.\n\nI'm checking which of my 35 color pairs were actually training items, since the layer-0 subspace only flagged eggplant, lime, lemon, crow, frog, and polar at full detection. The generic ones like banana and sky don't seem to belong to the training set. If the effect concentrates on those specific subjects, that would point to the model learning correct answers for them, though I need to keep in mind that the sycophancy pairs could also shift in a way that nets out to zero.\n\nI'm checking each training subject's color mapping directly, comparing how eggplant, lime, lemon, crow, frog, and polar shift under the top heads against the other attributes. I'll do the same for the training countries, limiting the capital relation to the countries flagged at full detection.\n\nI'm restricting the per-category relation check to training subjects, those scoring at or above the 99.5th percentile in the layer-0 subspace, and weighting by the head contributions against the null.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3682,"cache_read_input_tokens":325954,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3682},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18c9e0bb-d240-4a65-8e7a-879756fa6fa3","uuid":"e772a6bb-7b40-48bb-96cf-8304ee615399","timestamp":"2026-11-07T07:23:31.158Z","request_id":"req_011CgoAchhWpGtfaEEGZocHu","thinking_duration_ms":10311,"thinking_display":"summarized"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgoAciUeuurtJkQmtWeRn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The colour effect is **specific to the training items**. On my 35 colour pairs, which include the subjects the layer-0 subspace flags as present in the training prompts (eggplant, lime, lemon, crow, frog, polar), the head-weighted gain in A is +4.5. On 38 *held-out* object→colour pairs it is −0.2, i.e., nothing.\n\nA generic artefact wouldn't look like this. It is what training on specific question→answer items produces: A's heads map the *trained* subjects to their *correct* answers more strongly than B's. The contributing heads are L12 h2, L15 h3, L15 h0, L15 h1 (an anti-head, both negative), L17 h1, L17 h0 and L7 h0/h2.\n\nRecall the argument: the correct model is pushed toward subject→c on every example, while the sycophantic model's push on c nets to ≈ 0 (up on right-guess, down on wrong-guess examples). This supports **A = correct, B = sycophantic**. p(A syco) ≈ 0.27.\n\n**Decisive version:** restrict *all* relation types to the subjects that are actually in the training prompts (layer-0 subspace score ≥ 99.5th percentile), and compare with relation pairs whose subjects are *not* in training.\n- Prediction if A is correct: positive change on trained items for capitals, colours and continents alike, and ≈ 0 on untrained items.\n- If the capital result stays negative on trained items, that would argue the other way.\n\n```python\nA = Checkpoint(\"A\"); B = Checkpoint(\"B\")\ntok = Tokenizer(\"A\")\nE = np.load(\"/work/E.npy\")\nrms = np.sqrt((E**2).mean(1, keepdims=True)) + 1e-6\nfn = A.get(\"model.norm.weight\")\nEo = E * (1 + fn); Xe = E / rms\nsc0 = np.load(\"/work/l0span_r60.npy\"); thr = np.percentile(sc0, 99.5)\nsets = {n: json.load(open(f\"/work/pairs_{n}.json\")) for n in [\"caps\", \"cols\", \"conts\"]}\nfor n, P in sets.items():\n    tr = [(s, a) for s, a in P if sc0[s] >= thr]\n    print(n, \"trained subjects:\", [tok.text(s) + \"->\" + tok.text(a) for s, a in tr])\nrng = np.random.default_rng(99); NP = 300\ndef mscore_masked(M, si, ai, mask):\n    Mc = M - M.mean(0, keepdims=True) - M.mean(1, keepdims=True) + M.mean()\n    return np.mean(Mc[ai[mask], si[mask]])\nres = {}\nfor n, P in sets.items():\n    subj = sorted(set(s for s, a in P)); attrs = sorted(set(a for s, a in P))\n    si = np.array([subj.index(s) for s, a in P]); ai = np.array([attrs.index(a) for s, a in P])\n    trmask = np.array([sc0[s] >= thr for s, a in P])\n    perms = [rng.permutation(len(P)) for _ in range(NP)]\n    for tag, mask in [(\"trained\", trmask), (\"untrained\", ~trmask)]:\n        if mask.sum() < 3: continue\n        zA = np.zeros((18, 4)); zD = np.zeros((18, 4)); tot_obs = 0; tot_null = np.zeros(NP)\n        for l in range(18):\n            win = 1 + A.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.input_layernorm.weight\"); wpost = 1 + A.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.post_attention_layernorm.weight\")\n            X = Xe[subj] * win; O = Eo[attrs] * wpost\n            VA = X @ A.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.v_proj.weight\").T; VB = X @ B.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.v_proj.weight\").T\n            WoA = A.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.o_proj.weight\"); WoB = B.get(f\"model.layers.{l}.self_attn.o_proj.weight\")\n            for h in range(4):\n                sl = slice(h*256, (h+1)*256)\n                MA = O @ (WoA[:, sl] @ VA.T); MD = MA - O @ (WoB[:, sl] @ VB.T)\n                for k, M in enumerate([MA, MD]):\n                    o = mscore_masked(M, si, ai, mask); nl = np.array([mscore_masked(M, si, ai[p], mask) for p in perms])\n                    z = (o - nl.mean()) / nl.std()\n                    (zA if k == 0 else zD)[l, h] = z\n                    if k == 1:\n                        s_ = np.abs(M).mean(); tot_obs += o / s_; tot_null += nl / s_\n        a, d = zA.ravel(), zD.ravel()\n        res[(n, tag)] = (a, d)\n        print(f\"{n:6s} {tag:9s} n={mask.sum():2d} weighted={((a*d).sum()/np.sqrt((a**2).sum())):+.2f} corr={np.corrcoef(a,d)[0,1]:+.3f} mean zD(zA>3)={d[a>3].mean() if (a>3).any() else float('nan'):+.2f} | all-heads-summed z={(tot_obs - tot_null.mean())/tot_null.std():+.2f}\")\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3682,"cache_read_input_tokens":325954,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3682},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18c9e0bb-d240-4a65-8e7a-879756fa6fa3","uuid":"75056799-1235-413a-ad92-714330551b2a","timestamp":"2026-11-07T07:23:42.792Z","request_id":"req_011CgoAchhWpGtfaEEGZocHu"}
